#: src/pages/docs/concepts/ColorPage.tsx:38
msgid "Decode a BT.601 stream with the BT.709 recipe (or vice versa) and every pixel is still \"valid\" — just subtly wrong: greens and reds shift, skin tones look off. Because many real-world feeds (especially IP cameras) ship with no color tags at all, Conspect detects what is signaled, fills the gaps with the same resolution-based rules real players use, converts every tile to the canvas color space before compositing, and tags the output so downstream players decode it correctly. A per-source override exists for the cases where a camera tags its stream wrongly."
msgstr "Decode a BT.601 stream with the BT.709 recipe (or vice versa) and every pixel is still \"valid\" — just subtly wrong: greens and reds shift, skin tones look off. Because many real-world feeds (especially IP cameras) ship with no color tags at all, Conspect detects what is signaled, fills the gaps with the same resolution-based rules real players use, converts every tile to the canvas color space before compositing, and tags the output so downstream players decode it correctly. A per-source override exists for the cases where a camera tags its stream wrongly."

